#Level 6-7

Objective: The password for the next level is stored somewhere on the server and has all of the following properties:

owned by user bandit7
owned by group bandit6
33 bytes in size

Solution: For this part of the lab, since the password isn't located in a specific file location but somewhere on the entirety of the server, the best method to snuff it out is to use the 'find' command.
However simply writing the 'find' command with 'find . etc' won't cut it since the fullstop is used for file locations and not server side actions. So the best method I learned is to use the '/' instead of a fullstop.
So the command was 'find / -size 33c -group bandit6 -user bandit7' and then there will be a plethora of outputs which show 'Permission Denied' and so on, from these outputs we need to find the single one with the correct
location and no permissions denied. And upon finding the filepath we simply paste it and throw in the 'cat' command to get the password. There are 2 screenshots to show the files of permission denied and how to find the password.

#Level 7-8

Objective: The password for the next level is stored in the file data.txt next to the word millionth

Solution: Since we already have the name of the file, a basic grep command can work here with 'grep "millionth" data.txt' to get the answer. However if we have MULTIPLE files to find a needle in a haystack scenario,
another trick I learnt was to use the find command in conjunction with grep. So the command will look like 'find . -type f -exec grep "millionth" {} +' , where -exec acts as a bridge between the find and grep commands
as it searches through all files searching for that one specific keyword.

#Level 8-9

Objective: The password for the next level is stored in the file data.txt and is the only line of text that occurs only once

Solution: For this level the best way to find a unique word in the list of words that exist in data.txt is to use a combo of the sort and uniq commands. So upon further learning, 'sort data.txt | uniq -u' is the command
that I had come to understand to use. It sorts the values of the data.txt while choosing only the uniquely occuring value from a list of regurgitating values.

#Level 9-10

Objective: The password for the next level is stored in the file data.txt in one of the few human-readable strings, preceded by several ‘=’ characters.

Solution: Since this file stores information in human-readable strings, the 'strings' command is the best suited for pulling the readable aspect of the file. Furthermore since it is preceded by '=' signs, we use
grep along with it too. So the command I used is 'strings data.txt | grep "=" ' to pull the information from the file. 


#Level 10-11:

Objective: The password for the next level is stored in the file data.txt, which contains base64 encoded data

Solution: This was a personal favorite of mine, since the information text in data.txt is encoded in base64 syntax, our main objective is to DECODE the data into a human readable format, for this using the base64
command in conjunction with -d (or decode) is to be used. So the command we use is 'base64 -d data.txt'

#Level 11-12:

Objective: The password for the next level is stored in the file data.txt, where all lowercase (a-z) and uppercase (A-Z) letters have been rotated by 13 positions

Solution: This problem was a tad bit challenging as it involved using the 'tr' method in a way I hadn't known was possible. Basically, to solve this level,
we tackle a ROT13 (Rotate 13) substitution cipher by using the 'tr' command to map the scrambled alphabet back to its original form.
By executing cat data.txt | tr 'A-Za-z' 'N-ZA-Mn-za-m', we create a one-to-one translation table that shifts every character 13 places forward,
effectively "unwrapping" the alphabet starting from N for uppercase and n for lowercase. This command reverses the rotation by lining up the scrambled input 
against a correctly shifted output set, instantly transforming the gibberish found in the file into the human-readable password for the next level.

#Level 12-13:

Objective:The password for the next level is stored in the file data.txt, which is a hexdump of a file that has been repeatedly compressed. For this level it may be useful to create a directory 
under /tmp in which you can work. Use mkdir with a hard to guess directory name. Or better, use the command “mktemp -d”. Then copy the datafile using cp, and rename it using mv (read the manpages!)

Solution: Upon encountering this problem, I struggled a lot. First a regular cat command fails because it's in a hexdump format which makes the internal data look like a bunch of gibberish or hogwash.
The main issue in this file is that the data.txt file is compressed many times over .gz (gunzip) , .bz (bunzip) and .tar (POSIX tar) formats over and over again causing the file's ASCII contents to look 
like a trashcan of Hexadecimal dumps. The core objective it to make a directory (via the 'mktemp -d' command) then move into that directory  using cd and then copying the data.txt file (via the 'cp ~/data.txt .'
command). After doing that I made a silly mistake by renaming the file and making a new one using the 'mv data.txt new_file' command, but realized that it would fail because data.txt first needs to be converted
into a binary file. In order to do that, I used the xxd command namely 'xxd -r data.txt > new_file' where we convert the hexdump binary text file of data.txt into a real binary file. And upon doing that crucial step,
now begins the matryoshka doll unpeeling. First we need to identify the key type of file using the 'file new_file' command. If it is a gzip compressed data, then we need to rename the file and make a new one in the 
same format by compressing it using the 'mv new_file new_file.gz', and then we decompress it with the 'gunzip new_file.gz' command. Simultaenously we do the same for the .bz and .tar formats with 
Compression command: mv new_file new_file.bz , mv new_file new_file.tar
Decompression command: bunzip2 new_file.bz , tar -xf new_file.tar
Upon compressing and decompressing we need to simultaenously check the contents of the unpacked file that pops out to check the format (whether it is gzip, bzip or POSIX tar) and then compress it and decompress it
over and over until the final file is in an ASCII format. Then that file contains the password. 

